00:45:55.030 --> 00:45:57.090
<v Daniel D. Portado>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Daniel D. Portado>[西班牙语演讲]

00:45:57.090 --> 00:46:00.610
<v Rudy Rico>When you twist and dance to my mind control music.
<v Rudy Rico>当你扭动身体跳着我的思维控制音乐时。

00:46:00.610 --> 00:46:03.370
<v Daniel D. Portado>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Daniel D. Portado>[西班牙语演讲]

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:05.380
<v Rudy Rico>Screw the police.
<v Rudy Rico>去死吧，警察。

00:46:05.380 --> 00:46:07.480
<v Daniel D. Portado>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Daniel D. Portado>[西班牙语演讲]

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:09.450
<v Rudy Rico>Now go cook some food for my friends.
<v Rudy Rico>现在去为我的朋友煮点吃的。

00:46:09.450 --> 00:46:11.820
<v Daniel D. Portado>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Daniel D. Portado>[西班牙语演讲]

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:14.630
<v Rudy Rico>Stupid gringos, stupid gringos, stupid gringos.
<v Rudy Rico>蠢老美，蠢老美，蠢老美。

00:46:14.630 --> 00:46:21.070
<v Daniel D. Portado>So, as you can see, this is a very sinister device.
<v Daniel D. Portado>所以，正如你所见，这是一个非常邪恶的装置。

00:46:21.070 --> 00:46:29.240
<v Ira Glass>Daniel D. Portado, if you actually believe in deportation, what are you yourself still doing in California.
<v Ira Glass>丹尼尔·D·波塔多，如果你真的相信遣返，你自己为什么还留在加州？

00:46:29.240 --> 00:46:48.460
<v Daniel D. Portado>Well, I am here to help everyone get out. I hope to look forward to the day where I will stand at the border and say, will the last Mexican out of California, please turn out the lights. That will be me.
<v Daniel D. Portado>嗯，我在这里是为了帮助每个人离开。我希望期待有那么一天，我会站在边境上说，最后一个离开加州的墨西哥人，请关灯。那个人会是我。

00:46:48.460 --> 00:47:02.840
<v Ira Glass>Daniel D. Portado, chairman of Hispanics Against Liberal Takeover.
<v Ira Glass>丹尼尔·D·波塔多，“反对自由派接管的西班牙裔人”组织主席。

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:19.480
<v Ira Glass>Act  four-- Throwing Money at the Problem. Well, back in 1996, when the Democratic convention came to town here in Chicago, the city did a multi-million cleanup for the convention along the routes between the downtown hotels and the United Center, the sports arena where the convention was held.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕——“用钱砸向问题”。嗯，回到1996年，当民主党代表大会来到芝加哥时，市里在市中心酒店和联合中心（United Center，举行大会的体育场）之间的路线上进行了一次耗资数百万美元的清理活动。

00:47:19.480 --> 00:47:56.690
<v Ira Glass>Well one night, after the convention, I jumped in a cab with some other people, strangers, convention goers, and we sped up Monroe Street, them to their hotels, me, back to the radio station. The moon was out, the air was perfect, the street was freshly paved, and a woman from Washington DC, a lobbyist who had attended the convention in a sky box remarked, Chicago is so beautiful. There are no potholes, no homeless people, and the weather is so beautiful.
<v Ira Glass>大会结束后的一天晚上，我和其他人——陌生人、大会参加者——一起上了一辆出租车，我们沿着门罗街疾驰，他们去酒店，我回电台。月亮出来了，空气完美，街道是刚铺好的，一位来自华盛顿特区、参加了包厢会议的说客评论说：芝加哥太美了。没有坑洼，没有无家可归的人，天气也这么好。

00:47:56.690 --> 00:48:26.280
<v Ira Glass>Now, don't get me wrong, I love Chicago. But I have to say, we do have our fair share of potholes, homeless people, and bad weather. I knew, before this moment, that money could not buy love, but I had no idea it could buy this level of misperception. This next story is about what all the cameras and all the political elite see in the neighborhoods they drive through, and what they don't see.
<v Ira Glass>现在，别误会，我热爱芝加哥。但我不得不说，我们确实有相当多的坑洼、无家可归者和糟糕的天气。在那一刻之前，我就知道金钱买不到爱，但我没想到它能买到这种程度的误解。接下来的故事是关于所有的摄像机和所有的政治精英在他们开车经过的社区里看到了什么，以及他们没看到什么。

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:49.610
<v Ira Glass>You may remember the bestselling book, There Are No Children Here , from a few years back. In it, writer Alex Kotlowitz described the lives of two brothers at the Henry Horner homes. It was made into a TV film by Oprah Winfrey. It's been mentioned many times in speeches by Jack Kemp and by others. Well, Henry Horner is the public housing project that sits right across the street from the United Center where the convention was held.
<v Ira Glass>你可能还记得几年前那本畅销书《这里没有孩子》。在书中，作家亚历克斯·科特洛维茨（Alex Kotlowitz）描述了亨利·霍纳住宅区的两个兄弟的生活。它被奥普拉·温弗瑞（Oprah Winfrey）拍成了电视电影。杰克·坎普和其他人在演讲中多次提到它。嗯，亨利·霍纳就是那个公共住房项目，就位于举行大会的联合中心对面。

00:48:49.610 --> 00:49:11.350
<v Ira Glass>And when Chicago did its multimillion dollar cleanup for the Democratic convention, it cleaned up some of Horner. The boys from Alex Kotlowitz's book are now young men, and they do not live at Horner anymore. But they go back now and then. And during the convention, we asked one of them, Pharoah Walton, who was then 18, to give us a tour of what had changed at Horner because of the convention, and what hadn't changed.
<v Ira Glass>当芝加哥为民主党代表大会进行数百万美元的清理工作时，它也清理了霍纳的部分地区。科特洛维茨书中的男孩现在已经是年轻人了，他们不再住在霍纳了。但他们偶尔会回去。在大会期间，我们请其中一位法老·沃尔顿（Pharoah Walton，当时18岁）带领我们参观霍纳因为大会发生了什么变化，以及什么没变。

00:49:11.350 --> 00:49:32.470
<v Ira Glass>And one of the most interesting things about his report is how much of the neighborhood had improved for political and legal reasons, for all sorts of reasons, that never entered our political consciousness. They never became part of any discussion of what's going on in our cities. They never became part of the discussion of how things might be improved. So here, once again, is Pharoah's story.
<v Ira Glass>关于他的报道，最有趣的事情之一是，有多少社区为了政治和法律原因，为了各种各样的原因而得到了改善，这些原因从未进入我们的政治意识。它们从未成为任何关于我们城市正在发生什么事情的讨论的一部分。它们从未成为关于事情如何改善的讨论的一部分。所以，再次呈现法老的报道。

00:49:32.470 --> 00:50:12.660
<v Pharoah Walton>When I left Henry Horner five years ago, it looked like a ghost town. It had no green grass, broken windows, graffiti everywhere. Now, here in Horner, it's a different place. There are rows of flowers outside the maintenance building, and new windows in the office where my mother used to pay rent. There are trees, new elevators. Most of the changes are on the side of the building that faced the United Center. And on Washington Street, where the old Birney school used to be, there's a new playground, with a big lawn and a huge new blue and green jungle gym. Nicer than any playground I've ever seen in the city, even on the Northside, and everyone knows why.
<v Pharoah Walton>五年前我离开亨利·霍纳时，它看起来像个鬼城。没有绿草，窗户破碎，到处是涂鸦。现在，在这里，在霍纳，这是一个不同的地方。维护大楼外有成排的花朵，我母亲过去交租金的办公室也换了新窗户。有树木，有新电梯。大部分变化是在面对联合中心的那一侧建筑上。而在旧伯尼学校所在的华盛顿街，有一个新的游乐场，有大片草坪和巨大的蓝绿色新丛林健身架。比我在城里见过的任何游乐场都要好，甚至在北区也是，每个人都知道原因。

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:25.650
<v Little Girl>I like it. It's fun. I know why they did it. Because the President is coming. And then they all be taking kids and they going to be killing people, the President's soldiers.
<v Little Girl>我喜欢它。它很有趣。我知道他们为什么要这么做。因为总统要来了。然后他们都会带走孩子，总统的士兵将会杀人。

00:50:25.650 --> 00:50:32.410
<v Pharoah Walton>People say a lot of other things too. And not all of them make sense. Like what Kanue Howe told me about the playground.
<v Pharoah Walton>人们也说了许多其他事情。并不是所有这些话都有道理。比如卡努·豪（Kanue Howe）告诉我的关于游乐场的事。

00:50:32.410 --> 00:50:50.680
<v Kanue Howe>The Democratic convention coming, and now I can't be outside. [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. After 7:30 [UNINTELLIGIBLE] and if you're not, you're gonna be all [UNINTELLIGIBLE] all you home, then get a doctor. There be a lot of parties in our building and parties not supposed to be in our building.
<v Kanue Howe>民主党代表大会要来了，现在我不能在外面了。[无法辨认]。7:30以后[无法辨认]，如果你不在，你会在[无法辨认]，都在你家，然后找个医生。我们楼里会有很多派对，那些派对不应该在我们楼里。

00:50:50.680 --> 00:51:22.590
<v Pharoah Walton>People at Horner are glad about the physical changes. But most of them say they're mad that it took a President before the city would clean up a parking lot or plant a tree. Some things I'm hearing have changed and some things are still the same, like shootings and hot days. But when I was a kid, I would run in the house when the shootings start. Same with my nephew Snuggles and his friend Jeremy today.
<v Pharoah Walton>霍纳的人们对物理变化感到高兴。但他们大多数人说，他们很生气，因为直到总统到来，城市才愿意清理停车场或种一棵树。我听到有些事情改变了，有些事情依然如故，比如枪击和炎热的天气。但当我还是个孩子时，枪击开始时我会跑进屋里。今天我的侄子斯纳格尔斯（Snuggles）和他的朋友杰里米（Jeremy）也是一样。

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:24.990
<v Boy 1>They always trying to shoot on a hot day.
<v Boy 1>他们总是试图在炎热的日子里开枪。

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:28.650
<v Boy 2>And they start gang banging, when they on a hot day. And then all the kids got to go in the house.
<v Boy 2>当天气炎热时，他们就会开始团伙火拼。然后所有的孩子都必须进屋。

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:33.490
<v Boy 1>Like two weeks ago, some boy got shot, he got shot in the eye.
<v Boy 1>就像两周前，有个男孩被枪击了，他眼睛中弹了。

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:34.910
<v Boy 2>Probably is dead now.
<v Boy 2>现在可能已经死了。

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:48.590
<v Boy 1>He is dead. [UNINTELLIGIBLE] shooting all the time.
<v Boy 1>他死了。[无法辨认]一直在枪击。

00:51:48.590 --> 00:52:30.120
<v Pharoah Walton>Usually when you here about the projects, you hear that things are bad, and they're getting worse. But when I went to Horner, I heard a different story. There still shootings, still drugs, and still gangs. But, mostly, people told me that things have slowed down. In 1991, the residents of Henry Horner filed a lawsuit against the city and won. Because of that, the Chicago Housing Authority is cleaning it up, renovating old apartments, putting in new elevators, tearing down the worst building. They've moved 233 families out of Horner's in 1995 and installed 24 hour security guards. My friend Sylvia told me she feels safer.
<v Pharoah Walton>通常当你听说那些项目区时，你会听说情况很糟糕，而且正在恶化。但当我去霍纳时，我听到了一个不同的故事。那里仍然有枪击、毒品和团伙。但是，大多数人告诉我，情况已经放缓了。1991年，亨利·霍纳的居民对市政府提起了诉讼并赢了。因此，芝加哥住房管理局正在清理它，翻新旧公寓，安装新电梯，拆除最坏的建筑。他们在1995年将233个家庭搬出了霍纳，并安装了24小时保安。我的朋友西尔维娅（Sylvia）告诉我，她觉得更安全了。

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:46.840
<v Sylvia>So it's doing a lots better since 20 years it was looking around here, so. Crime [UNINTELLIGIBLE] a lot better too. They ain't doing too much shooting or nothing, so that's good. Police are around a little more, so that's better too. Since they put that United Center up there, because they  watch that a lot.
<v Sylvia>所以比起20年前这附近的样子，它好多了，所以。犯罪[无法辨认]也好了很多。他们没怎么开枪什么的，所以那很好。警察在这附近多了一点，所以那也更好。自从他们在那儿盖了那个联合中心，因为他们经常看着那儿。

00:52:46.840 --> 00:53:04.420
<v Pharoah Walton>Sylvia says police are always driving around the neighborhood since the United Center was built. I visited my grandmother, and she said the same thing. That things are getting better. We sat in her apartment with gospel music on the radio and a preacher on the television. And she told me about life in Horner now.
<v Pharoah Walton>西尔维娅说自从联合中心建成后，警察总是在社区周围巡逻。我拜访了我的祖母，她也说了同样的话。情况正在好转。我们坐在她的公寓里，听着收音机里的福音音乐，电视上放着牧师的节目。她告诉了我现在在霍纳的生活。

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:38.310
<v Grandmother>Well, it's not too bad around here. I never read about nothing happening around here, like somebody getting killed in a drive-by shooting. It's not too bad. I walk out this house every day and goes off. Lights be out, I have to take a flashlight, but I'm not afraid. I'm not afraid. Of course, I got God, first of all.
<v Grandmother>嗯，这附近不太糟。我从来没读到过这附近发生什么事，比如有人在得来速枪击中丧生。不太糟。我每天走出这个屋子，然后出去。灯灭了，我得带手电筒，但我并不害怕。我不害怕。当然，首先，我有上帝。

00:53:38.310 --> 00:54:02.030
<v Pharoah Walton>One of the things that the President and the delegates at the convention won't see when they look at Horner from across the United Center parking lot, is The Boys & Girls Club. Physically The Boys & Girls Club hasn't changed since I was little. Old pool tables, a basketball court, cinderblock wall, it's not in great shape. I talked to the grandfather of the community, Major Adam.
<v Pharoah Walton>总统和大会代表从联合中心停车场对面看霍纳时，他们看不到的一件事是男孩女孩俱乐部（The Boys & Girls Club）。从物理上讲，男孩女孩俱乐部从我小时候起就没变过。旧台球桌、篮球场、煤渣砖墙，状况不是很好。我采访了社区的祖父，亚当少校（Major Adam）。

00:54:02.030 --> 00:54:24.290
<v Pharoah Walton>He's worked at the Boys & Girls Club for 40 years. Everybody knows him, and everybody respects him. Kids would listen to Major before they'd listen to teacher, or parents, or even a police. He's the only person at Horner who can walk into the middle of a gang fight and make it stop. In fact, he's famous for jumping into the middle of fights. And Major would be the first person to tell you about all the fights he's stopped.
<v Pharoah Walton>他在男孩女孩俱乐部工作了40年。每个人都知道他，每个人都尊重他。孩子们在听老师、家长甚至警察之前，会先听少校的。他是霍纳唯一一个能走进团伙火拼中间并让它停止的人。事实上，他以跳进战斗中而闻名。少校会是第一个告诉你他制止了多少战斗的人。

00:54:24.290 --> 00:54:53.290
<v Major Adam>He's going to jump on eight guys with these three knives. I didn't know he had three knives. But he had one in his hand he was going to get these guys. So I had to grab this guy and take this knife away from him. While the guy came out there, 12 of them was jumping on one guy. And it made me so mad, I said, well look, I'm going to take all 12 of you guys, one at a time. And I'd take them one at a time, and I whooped all 12 of them. I'm gonna do do it by myself. I picked them up and throwed them over the fence. I'm telling you, you got--
<v Major Adam>他要用这三把刀跳向八个人。我不知道他有三把刀。但他手里有一把，他要干掉这些人。所以我不得不抓住这家伙并从他手中夺走这把刀。当那家伙出来时，他们中有12个人正围攻一个人。我火冒三丈，我说，好吧，听着，我要干掉你们12个，一次一个。我一次一个，我打败了他们12个。我要自己搞定。我把他们提起来扔过栅栏。我告诉你，你必须——

00:54:53.290 --> 00:55:22.860
<v Pharoah Walton>Some people say the neighborhood is safer because over 200 families moved out. There are fewer people to get in trouble or shoot each other. My friend Sylvia says, people have less time to fool around, because the Housing Authority is hiring residents to do construction and clean the place up. Another friend of mine says, people are getting their acts together, because now the city is kicking people out and tearing buildings down, and they don't want to lose their homes. But the way Major sees it, the neighborhood is better, because there are people in Horner who are trying to make a difference.
<v Pharoah Walton>有些人说社区更安全了，因为200多个家庭搬走了。惹麻烦或互相射击的人少了。我的朋友西尔维娅说，人们没有时间瞎混，因为住房管理局雇佣居民进行施工和清理工作。我的另一个朋友说，人们正变得正经起来，因为现在城市在赶人走、拆楼，他们不想失去家园。但按照少校的看法，社区变好了，因为在霍纳有些人正试图带来改变。

00:55:22.860 --> 00:55:49.080
<v Major Adam>I'll tell you, crime have went down in our neighborhood, this neighborhood, because you have a guy like me working in the neighborhood. When you walk in there, a lot of them gang bangers just came, I sent them. So is a lot of things I do for them. I let them play basketball. When they want to go back to school, I see that they go back to school. They go to jail, I send them money and stuff like that.
<v Major Adam>我告诉你，犯罪在我们的社区，这个社区，下降了，因为有个像我这样的人在社区工作。当你走进去时，很多混混都来了，我遣送了他们。所以我为他们做了很多事。我让他们打篮球。当他们想回学校时，我确保他们回学校。他们进监狱，我给他们寄钱之类的事。

00:55:49.080 --> 00:56:08.020
<v Pharoah Walton>Like my Aunt Millie says, you can't beautify the outside, when the inside ain't right. Major's working hard to beautify the inside.
<v Pharoah Walton>就像我的米莉阿姨（Aunt Millie）说的，当里面不好时，你不能美化外面。少校正努力美化里面。

00:56:08.020 --> 00:56:26.900
<v Pharoah Walton>In the main room at The Boys & Girls Club, there's a trophy case. Inside there are all the trophies won by Horner baseball teams, basketball teams, and football teams. And pinned to the back of the trophy case, there are pictures of Horner residents who've made something of their lives, who've gotten out of the project, went on to do great things.
<v Pharoah Walton>在男孩女孩俱乐部的主厅里，有一个奖杯陈列柜。里面放着霍纳棒球队、篮球队和橄榄球队赢得的所有奖杯。奖杯柜背面贴着霍纳居民的照片，他们成就了自己的生活，走出了项目区，去做了伟大的事情。

00:56:26.900 --> 00:56:30.860
<v Major Adam>All three of those young men up t there are teachers. They grew up in the area.
<v Major Adam>在那上面的那三个年轻人都是老师。他们是在这个地区长大的。

00:56:30.860 --> 00:56:31.660
<v Pharoah Walton>All three of them?
<v Pharoah Walton>他们三个都是？

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:44.550
<v Major Adam>Yeah, all three there. And this is Dr. Steven Parker at Chicago State. That's Verdine from Earth, Wind & Fire.
<v Major Adam>是的，那三个都是。这是芝加哥州立大学的史蒂文·帕克（Steven Parker）博士。那是“地球、风与火”乐队（Earth, Wind & Fire）的维丁（Verdine）。

00:56:44.550 --> 00:57:08.530
<v Pharoah Walton>Then he pointed out a blank spot, and asked me for a picture. His way of saying, he knew I was going to make it.
<v Pharoah Walton>然后他指着一个空白处，向我要一张照片。他用这种方式表示，他知道我会成功的。

00:57:08.530 --> 00:57:29.080
<v Ira Glass>Well, This American Life is produced by Alex Blumberg, Susan Burton, Blue Chevigny, Julie Snyder, Nancy Updike, and me. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margie Rochlin, Alix Spiegel and consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Starlee Kine. Some work in todays show was produced by Peter Clowney and Alix Spiegel. Musical help today from John Connors, Steve Cushing, and Anaheed Alani.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，《美国生活》由亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）、布鲁·切维尼（Blue Chevigny）、朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）、南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）和我制作。特约编辑保罗·塔夫（Paul Tough）、杰克·希特（Jack Hitt）、玛吉·罗克林（Margie Rochlin）、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔（Alix Spiegel）和顾问莎拉·沃威尔（Sarah Vowell）。托德·巴赫曼（Todd Bachmann）和斯塔莉·基恩（Starlee Kine）提供制作帮助。今天节目中的一些工作由彼得·克劳尼（Peter Clowney）和阿利克斯·斯皮格尔制作。今天来自约翰·康纳斯（John Connors）、史蒂夫·库欣（Steve Cushing）和阿纳希德·阿拉尼（Anaheed Alani）的音乐帮助。

